        "original_id": "e79f4d4e-7a6b-5f75-afdb-6557dbb43471",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Alexander Glazunov, Marius Petipa, John Landor, Ilya Musin\n- Works: Symphony No. 9 in D minor, Raymonda\n- Organizations: Saint Petersburg Conservatory, Petrograd Conservatory, Leningrad Conservatory, Oxford University, Royal Academy of Music\n- Topics: conducting studies, ballet, symphony composition\n\nFacts:\n- In 1905, Alexander Glazunov began serving as director of the Saint Petersburg Conservatory.\n- Raymonda is a ballet in three acts and four scenes with an apotheosis, choreographed by Marius Petipa, with music by Alexander Glazunov, and it is Glazunov's Opus 57.\n- In 1910, Alexander Glazunov began composing Symphony No. 9 in D minor.\n- Following the Bolshevik Revolution, Alexander Glazunov helped reorganize the Saint Petersburg Conservatory into the Petrograd Conservatory and then the Leningrad Conservatory.\n- John Landor studied at Oxford University, the Royal Academy of Music in London, and the St Petersburg Conservatory under conducting teacher Ilya Musin.\n- In 1928, Alexander Glazunov's service as director of the Saint Petersburg Conservatory ended.\n- In 1936, Alexander Glazunov died with Symphony No. 9 in D minor still unfinished.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "e674330f-28ac-5e17-b71f-9ced1a33ef53",
        "text": "Page: Symphony No. 9 (Glazunov). Sentence: Alexander Glazunov's Symphony No. 9 in D minor was begun in 1910, but was still unfinished by the time of his death in 1936.\nPage: Alexander Glazunov. Sentence: He served as director of the Saint Petersburg Conservatory between 1905 and 1928 and was instrumental in the reorganization of the institute into the Petrograd Conservatory, then the Leningrad Conservatory, following the Bolshevik Revolution.\nPage: Raymonda. Sentence: Raymonda (Russian: Раймонда ) is a ballet in three acts, four scenes with an apotheosis, choreographed by Marius Petipa, with music by Alexander Glazunov, his opus 57.\nPage: John Landor. Sentence: He studied at Oxford University, the Royal Academy of Music in London and the St Petersburg Conservatory under conducting teacher Ilya Musin.",
